    "## 1 — Paper Methodology\n",
    "\n",
    "Document exactly what the paper did before implementing anything. All details below are taken directly from Fang et al. (2023). Cross-reference with the paper when populating."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "48ecaf1a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 1.1 Endpoints reported"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "83ac50aa",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "HLM - Human Liver Microsomal stability, reported as intrinsic clearance (CLint, mL/min/kg) \n",
    "\n",
    "MDR1-MDCK ER (Efflux Ratio)\n",
    "\n",
    "Solubility at pH 6.8 (ug/mL)\n",
    "\n",
    "RLM - Rat Liver Microsomal stability, reported as intrinsic clearance (CLint, mL/min/kg) \n",
    "\n",
    "hPPB - human plasma protein binding, percent unbound\n",
    "\n",
    "rPPB - rat plasma protein binding, percent unbound"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "cecea909",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 1.2 Featurization"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ecdb40aa",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "For featurization, they used a few methods:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. RDkit 2d descriptors (the paper said they used 316 on v2018.09.3.0, my version has 210 in Descriptors.descList, with descriptastorus selecting 200 of those, dropping 10 unstable/redundant ones), measurable and estimated physiochemical properties, continous.\n",
    "\n",
    "Reviewing their soure code: https://github.com/molecularinformatics/Computational-ADME/blob/main/ML/ADME_ML_public.py they used, they called their descriptor set \"rdMolDes\", I copied their MDlist as included it as part of my features as.\n",
    "\n",
    "Note: They normalize these descriptors for SVM and Lasso, FCNN\n",
    "\n",
    "2. FPFC4 note NOT ECFP4. 2d structural fingerprints, binary, vector, 1024 bits, r=4 (according to the paper, BUT line 187 in ADME_ML_public.py reveals they used radius = 2, so may have to test both to recreate their results, will follow the code rather than the paper), useFeatures=True\n",
    "\n",
    "For SVM and Lasso, their code in ADME_ML_public.py shows they used Robubst scaler, fit and transform (separate steps for some reason on X_train instead of doing it in one go with fit_transform).\n",
    "\n",
    "Note:\n",
    "\n",
    "MPNN1 (without RDkit descriptors)\n",
    "MPNN2 (with RDkit descriptors) uses rdkit_2d_normalized, see line 34 in MPNN_public.py along with \"--no_features_scaling\"\n",
    "\n",
    "3.  RDkit 2d descriptrs + FPFC4 concatenated\n",
    "\n",
    "4. rdkit_2d_normalized Note: Not the same as rdMolDes normalized"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "25bd47e1",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 1.3 Split strategy"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3bc0bb59",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The paper used temporal splits for their in house ADME dataset, for their public dataset which is a combination of:\n",
    "\n",
    "Enamine, eMolecules, WuXi LabNetwork, Mcule datasets, covering 3521 compounds. - This is a source of inter-lab noise (they also added CHEMBL) \n",
    "\n",
    "I believe they used random splits (see shuffle and train_test_split in their and my code). \n",
    "\n",
    "They used grid search and cross validation with a fold of k = 5 (RepeatedKFold) and 3 repeats to determine pearson r for 15 splits for validation, regardless of whether they were hyperparamter tuning or not. \n",
    "\n",
    "The public dataset does not have a time index so temporal splits are not possible with it, scaffold splits are possible but they didnt implement it, something I will do later after adding the other models/hyper param tuning."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e2f1a745",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
