    "### 4.1a — What the descriptor inputs look like, and why each model gets a different scaling\n",
    "\n",
    "Before training on the `rdkit` featureset, it's worth *looking* at it. It's 316 RDKit `rdMolDes`\n",
    "descriptors — real-valued molecular properties (atom counts, molecular weight, surface-area bins, 3D\n",
    "shape moments). Two things make them awkward to feed a model raw, and Panels A–B below confirm both:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **They live on wildly different scales** — one column ranges 0–2, another 0–10,000.\n",
    "- **Many are heavy-tailed** — a handful of unusually large molecules sit far out in the tail.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why only this featureset?** Scaling is a live choice *only* for continuous, multi-scale features, so\n",
    "the rmoldes set is the only one worth inspecting here. The fingerprint featuresets (FCFP4 / ECFP4) are\n",
    "binary {0, 1} — already bounded and on one common scale, so skew / kurtosis / scale don't apply (their\n",
    "relevant question is *bit sparsity*, not scaling). `rdkit_2d_normalized` (the 200-descriptor MPNN3 set)\n",
    "is CDF-bounded to [0, 1] *by construction*, so there's no decision left to inspect. And the un-normalized\n",
    "`rdkit_2d_raw` (MPNN4's input) is unbounded just like rmoldes — it would only re-tell the same story.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why that matters depends on the model:**\n",
    "\n",
    "- *Trees (RF, XGBoost, LightGBM)* split on \"is this feature above a threshold?\", so rescaling a column\n",
    "  changes nothing. They get the **raw** data.\n",
    "- *Linear / kernel models (SVM, Lasso, BayesianRidge)* weigh features against each other, so one column\n",
    "  with a huge range would drown out the rest. **RobustScaler** (subtract the median, divide by the IQR)\n",
    "  fixes the scale and shrugs off outliers — but it's a *linear* shift-and-stretch, so a point 40 IQRs out\n",
    "  in the raw data is still 40 units out afterwards.\n",
    "- *Neural nets* are the fussy case: a few extreme inputs can swamp the network's internals. What they want\n",
    "  is **bounded** inputs. ChemProp's own `rdkit_2d_normalized` gets there by ranking each descriptor into\n",
    "  [0, 1]. For **MPNN2** we do the same with `QuantileTransformer(uniform)` — rank every value and map it to\n",
    "  [0, 1], which squashes the tails flat. That's the closest match to MPNN3, so the two stay comparable.\n",
    "\n",
    "Summary of who gets what:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Model(s) on `rdkit` | Scaling | In one line |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| RF, XGBoost, LightGBM | raw | trees don't care about scale |\n",
    "| SVM, Lasso, BayesianRidge | RobustScaler | need a shared scale; tolerate its linear tails |\n",
    "| **FCNN** | RobustScaler | a neural net — but *not* bounded (see flag) |\n",
    "| MPNN2 | QuantileTransformer → [0, 1] | bounded, tails squashed, matches MPNN3 |\n",
